      "link": "https://english.pravda.ru/news/world/171077-ammonia-leaks-dos-pinos-plant-safety-demands/",
      "title": "Union Demands Safety Overhaul After Ammonia Leaks at Costa Rican Ice Cream Plant",
      "description": "Two ammonia leaks occurred at the Dos Pinos ice cream plant in the coastal district of Coyola, Alajuela, on Sunday, October 25, and Monday, October 26. The union representing the workers, Union de Empleados de la Cooperativa Dos Pinos (Udeco), reported that four employees required emergency medical attention: three women who felt ill during evacuation and a mechanic who sustained a hand burn. Udeco stated that the first incident began around 9:00 a. m. on Sunday during maintenance of equipment unit number 304. The union claimed that emergency response personnel were absent from the area, forcing staff to manage the evacuation themselves. The second incident occurred around 10:20 a. m. on Monday, following the rupture of a valve during technical intervention. According to the union, the mechanic was working without personal protective equipment, which resulted in an ammonia cloud forming in the production zone. A central issue in the union's complaint is the absence of dedicated ammonia detection systems. Udeco noted that while the facility is equipped with standard smoke detectors and fire alarms, it lacks devices that specifically detect gas refrigerant leaks. The union also cited blocked evacuation routes, claiming one exit was locked and another obstructed by stacked pallets and cargo. Witness accounts collected by Udeco indicate that some staff had to use alternative routes to leave the danger zone, and one worker was temporarily trapped while attempting to exit through a service door.",
